At high altitude the atmospheric pressure is low, so the body does not get enough oxygen. A person who goes out at once can suffer from **altitude sickness** (nausea, fatigue and heart palpitations). Over the next couple of days the body gradually acclimatises by compensating for the low oxygen availability (any two):
- **increased production of red blood cells (RBCs)**, so that more haemoglobin is available to carry oxygen;
- **decrease in the binding affinity of haemoglobin** for oxygen, so that haemoglobin releases oxygen to the tissues more readily;
- **increase in the breathing rate**, so that more air, and so more oxygen, is taken in.

Once these changes have taken place the person no longer feels sick, which is why tourists are advised to rest for at least two days before venturing out.
